# =============================================================================
# Custom detection rule - Friday Working-Hours (CIC-IDS2017) HTTP request flood
# Triage of logs/friday-validate-new-rules/eve.json (data/pcaps/Friday-WorkingHours.pcap)
#
# RE-MEASURED for this validation pass. Whole-capture counts, filter
# src_ip=172.16.0.1 AND event_type=http:
#   94,613 HTTP transactions 172.16.0.1 -> 192.168.10.50 (94,570 of them to
#   :80, 94,548 answered HTTP 200 OK), 2017-07-08 04:26:37 - 04:47:xx.
#   Peak minute 04:36 = 5,047 transactions (~84 req/s), typical minute ~4,850
#   (~81 req/s), 21-minute mean ~75 req/s. The rev:1 header claimed
#   189,188 GETs / "~9,500-10,000 requests/min" - that figure is not supported
#   by this capture; the measured rate is ~4.8k/min.
#
# WHY rev:2 - the rev:1 threshold (5000 requests / 60 s = 83.3 req/s) sat ABOVE
# the sustained rate of the very campaign it was written for. Validated by
# temporarily loading two replicas into a rule file that IS in the run config
# (wednesday_slowloris.rules) and re-running the full Friday pcap
# (logs/validate-9500003-threshold/):
#   sid:9500097 (replica of rev:1: http GET "/", 5000/60s)  -> fired 4x
#        (04:32:31, 04:34:21, 04:36:26, 04:43:24) i.e. only when the busiest
#        minutes clear 83 req/s; silent for the other ~17 minutes of the flood.
#   sid:9500098 (rev:2 candidate: method-agnostic, 6000/120s) -> fired 10x,
#        evenly spread 04:27:59 - 04:45:47 across the whole flood.
# Both replicas were removed after validation; wednesday_slowloris.rules was
# restored byte-for-byte.
#
# rev:2 is deliberately NOT method-restricted ("content:HTTP", no http.method),
# so POST/HEAD/random-verb floods are also caught; the GET-specific companion
# rule sid:9600001 in friday_ddos_portscan.rules (GET, 5000/120s, fired 11x on
# this capture) remains the higher-fidelity signal for plain GET floods.
# Overlap on a GET flood is expected - deploy either rule for that case, or both
# for method-agnostic coverage, but do not treat the two alerts as independent.
#
# NOTE: this file is NOT in config/suricata.yaml's rule-files list, so it is
# dead code until the engineer adds it there (see report, section 5).
# =============================================================================

alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET 80 ( \
    msg:"CUSTOM HTTP Request Flood - sustained high-volume request flood to HOME_NET web server"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    content:"HTTP"; nocase; \
    threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 6000, seconds 120; \
    classtype:attempted-dos; \
    sid:9500003; rev:2;)
